    <p>
      If you have ever tried to lose weight, you have likely encountered the terrifying warning of"starvation mode." Online diet forums and fitness coaches frequently claim that if you drop your calories too low, your body will panic, lock onto fat reserves, and completely halt your weight loss—or worse, cause you to magically gain body fat from thin air.
    </p>
    <p>
      Does human physiology actually behave this way? The scientific answer is unequivocally <strong>no</strong>."Starvation mode," as sensationalized in pop culture, violates the laws of thermodynamics. However, the real physiological event people experience when weight loss stalls is <strong>metabolic adaptation</strong> (clinically known as adaptive thermogenesis).
    </p>
    <p>
      Understanding the critical difference between the myth of starvation mode and the reality of metabolic adaptation is the secret to overcoming stubborn weight loss plateaus without crashing your metabolism or giving up on your goals.
    </p>

    <div class="bg-brand-soft border-l-4 border-brand p-4 my-8 rounded-r-lg">
